{
  "image_name": "2d05390769a051d057a8477dc0f54dcd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several examples of how nuclear condensates or protein assemblies regulate gene expression and cell behavior in immune cells. Across the panels, different cell types use distinct nuclear compartments to organize RNA or chromatin factors and thereby control transcriptional programs.\n\nOne example shows hematopoietic stem cells, where the chemokine gene Ccl5 appears to be silenced by associating its mRNA with SON-containing nuclear speckles, consistent with reduced Ccl5 expression during stem-cell self-renewal. Another schematic shows chromatin-opening factors, including EBF1 and BRG1, working with FUS to convert closed chromatin into a more open, clustered state that would favor transcriptional accessibility.\n\nIn activated T cells, a nucleolar complex involving DCAF13, UTP23, NPM1, and 18S rRNA is depicted as promoting ribosome biogenesis, which corresponds to increased ribosome production and T-cell proliferation. In activated macrophages, a transcription-related assembly containing NF-κB, ZMYND8, and LSD1 is shown at enhancer-like chromatin marked by H3K4 methylation and eRNA/Pol II activity; the outcome is reduced inflammatory gene expression. Overall, the figure conveys that spatially organized nuclear assemblies can either activate or repress key immune-cell programs by controlling RNA handling, chromatin structure, and ribosome production.",
